nformation or personal data.\nL31: \nL32: [Input]\nL33:   * cite0†☰ L34:   *     *     * cite1†Home L35:     * Browse\nL36:       * cite2†Titles L37:       * cite3†Agencies L38:     * CFR Updates\nL39:       * cite4†Recent Changes L40:       * cite5†Issue Summaries L41:       * cite6†Corrections L42:     * Finding Aids\nL43:       * cite7†Parallel Table of Authorities L44:       * cite8†Subject Index L45:       * cite9†Agency-prepared Indexes L46:     * Reader Aids\nL47:       * cite10†Reader Aids Home L48:       * cite11†Using the eCFR Point-in-Time System L49:       * cite12†Understanding the eCFR L50:       * cite13†Government Policy and OFR Procedures L51:       * cite14†Developer Resources L52:       * cite15†Recent Site Updates L53:     * My eCFR\nL54:       * cite16†Sign In / Sign Up L55:     * [Input] [Input: Search the eCFR]\nL56: # cite1†eCFR L57: \nL58: ## The Electronic Code of Federal Regulations\nL59: \nL60: ###### Enhanced Content :: FR Reference\nL61: \nL62: Enhanced content is provided to the user to provide additional context.\nL63: \nL64: ###### Enhanced Content :: FR Reference\nL65: # Title 47\nL66: \nL67: This content is from the eCFR and is authoritative but unofficial.\nL68: \nL69: Displaying title 47, up to date as of 9/28/2026. Title 47 was last amended 9/25/2026.\nL70: \nL71: cite0†view historical versions L72: \nL73: A cite17†drafting site is available†drafting.ecfr.gov for use when drafting amendatory language\nL74: \nL75: cite17†switch to drafting site†drafting.ecfr.gov L76: \nL77: New Finding Aids are now available on eCFR.gov. The Parallel Table of Authorities, Subject Index, and Agency-prepared Indexes are now online! cite18†Learn more L78: Navigate by entering citations or phrases (eg: 1 CFR 1.1 49 CFR 172.101 Organization and Purpose 1/1.1 Regulation Y FAR).\nL79: \nL80: Choosing an item from citations and headings will bring you directly to the content. Choosing an item from full text search results will bring you to those results. Pressing enter in the search box will also bring you to search results.\nL81: \nL82: Background and more details are available in the cite19†Search & Navigation guide.\nL83: \nL84: [Input] [Input] [Input]\nL85: [Input: Enter a search term or CFR reference (eg. fishing or 1 CFR 1.1)]\nL86: \nL87: [Input: Enter a search term or CFR reference (eg. fishing or 1 CFR 1.1)]\nL88: \nL89:   *   * Table of Contents\nL90:   * Details\nL91:   * Print/PDF\nL92:   * Display Options\nL93:   *   * Timeline\nL94:   * Go to Date\nL95:   * Compare Dates\nL96:   *   * Published Edition\nL97:   *   * Developer Tools\nL98: ###### eCFR Content\nL99: \nL100: The Code of Federal Regulations (CFR) is the official legal print publication containing the codification of the general and permanent rules published in the Federal Register by the departments and agencies of the Federal Government. The Electronic Code of Federal Regulations (eCFR) is a continuously updated online version of the CFR. It is not an official legal edition of the CFR.\nL101: \nL102: cite20†Learn more about the eCFR, its status, and the editorial process.\nL103: ###### Enhanced Content\nL104: \nL105: View table of contents for this page.\nL106: \nL107: ## Subpart P—Competitive Availability of Navigation Devices\nL108: \nL109: #### Source:\nL110: \nL111: cite21†63 FR 38094†www.federalregister.gov , July 15, 1998, unless otherwise noted.\nL112: #### § 76.1200 Definitions.\nL113: \nL114: As used in this subpart:\nL115: (a) Multichannel video programming system. A distribution system that makes available for purchase, by customers or subscribers, multiple channels of video programming other than an open video system as defined by cite22†§ 76.1500(a) . Such systems include, but are not limited to, cable television systems, BRS/EBS systems, direct broadcast satellite systems, other systems for providing direct-to-home multichannel video programming via satellite, and satellite master antenna systems.\nL116: (b) Multichannel video programming distributor. A person such as, but not limited to, a cable operator, a BRS/EBS provider, a direct broadcast satellite service, or a television receive-only satellite program distributor, who owns or operates a multichannel video programming system.\nL117: (c) Navigation devices. Devices such as converter boxes, interactive communications equipment, and other equipment used by consumers to access multichannel video programming and other services offered over multichannel video programming systems.\nL118: \nL119: (d) Affiliate. A person or entity that (directly or indirectly) owns or controls, is owned or controlled by, or is under common ownership or control with, another person, as defined in the notes accompanying cite23†§ 76.501 .\nL120: (e) Conditional access. The mechanisms that provide for selective access and denial of specific services and make use of signal security that can prevent a signal from being received except by authorized users.\nL121: \nL122: [cite21†63 FR 38094†www.federalregister.gov , July 15, 1998, as amended at cite24†69 FR 72046†www.federalregister.gov , Dec. 10, 2004]\nL123: #### § 76.1201 Rights of subscribers to use or attach navigation devices.\nL124: \nL125: No multichannel video programming distributor shall prevent the connection or use of navigation devices to or with its multichannel video programming system, except in those circumstances where electronic or physical harm would be caused by the attachment or operation of such devices or such devices may be used to assist or are intended or designed to assist in the unauthorized receipt of service.\nL126: #### § 76.1202 Availability of navigation devices.\nL127: \nL128: No multichannel video programming distributor shall by contract, agreement, patent right, intellectual property right or otherwise prevent navigation devices that do not perform conditional access or security functions from being made available to subscribers from retailers, manufacturers, or other vendors that are unaffiliated with such owner or operator, subject to cite25†§ 76.1209 .\nL129: #### § 76.1203 Incidence of harm.\nL130: A multichannel video programming distributor may restrict the attachment or use of navigation devices with its system in those circumstances where electronic or physical harm would be caused by the attachment or operation of such devices or such devices that assist or are intended or designed to assist in the unauthorized receipt of service.\nL131: Such restrictions may be accomplished by publishing and providing to subscribers standards and descriptions of devices that may not be used with or attached to its system. Such standards shall foreclose the attachment or use only of such devices as raise reasonable and legitimate concerns of electronic or physical harm or theft of service. In any situation where theft of service or harm occurs or is likely to occur, service may be discontinued.\nL132: #### § 76.1204 Availability of equipment performing conditional access or security functions.\nL133: \nL134: (a)\nL135: \nL136: (1) A multichannel video programming distributor that utilizes Navigation Devices to perform conditional access functions shall make available equipment that incorporates only the conditional access functions of such devices.\nL137: \nL138: (2) The foregoing requirement shall not apply to a multichannel video programming distributor that supports the active use by subscribers of Navigation Devices that:\nL139: (i) Operate throughout the continental United States, and\nL140: \nL141: (ii) Are available from retail outlets and other vendors throughout the United States that are not affiliated with the owner or operator of the multichannel video programming system.\nL142: (b) Conditional access function equipment made available pursuant to cite26†paragraph (a)(1) of this section shall be designed to connect to and function with other Navigation Devices available through the use of a commonly used interface or an interface that conforms to appropriate technical standards promulgated by a national standards organization.\nL143: (c) No multichannel video programming distributor shall by contract, agreement, patent, intellectual property right or otherwise preclude the addition of features or functions to the equipment made available pursuant to this section that are not designed, intended or function to defeat the conditional access controls of such devices or to provide unauthorized access to service.\nL144: \nL145: (d) Notwithstanding the foregoing, Navigation Devices need not be made available pursuant to this section where:\nL146: (1) It is not reasonably feasible to prevent such devices from being used for the unauthorized reception of service; or\nL147: \nL148: (2) It is not reasonably feasible to separate conditional access from other functions without jeopardizing security.\nL149: \nL150: (e) cite26†Paragraphs (a)(1) , cite27†(b) , and cite28†(c) of this section shall not apply to the provision of any Navigation Device that:\nL151: \nL152: (1) Employs conditional access mechanisms only to access analog video programming;\nL153: (2) Is capable only of providing access to analog video programming offered over a multichannel video programming distribution system; and\nL154: \nL155: (3) Does not provide access to any digital transmission of multichannel video programming or any other digital service through any receiving, decoding, conditional access, or other function, including any conversion of digital programming or service to an analog format.\nL156: \nL157: [cite29†81 FR 13997†www.federalregister.gov , Mar. 16, 2016]\nL158: #### § 76.1205 Availability of interface information.\nL159: \nL160: Technical information concerning interface parameters that are needed to permit navigation devices to operate with multichannel video programming systems shall be provided by the system operator upon request in a timely manner.\nL161: \nL162: [cite30†85 FR 78239†www.federalregister.gov , Dec. 4, 2020]\nL163: #### § 76.1206 Equipment sale or lease charge subsidy prohibition.\nL164: \nL165: Multichannel video programming distributors offering navigation devices subject to the provisions of cite31†§ 76.923 for sale or lease directly to subscribers, shall adhere to the standards reflected therein relating to rates for equipment and installation and shall separately state the charges to consumers for such services and equipment.\nL166: #### § 76.1207 Waivers.\nL167: The Commission may waive a regulation adopted under this subpart for a limited time, upon an appropriate showing by a provider of multichannel video programming and other services offered over multichannel video programming systems, or an equipment provider that such a waiver is necessary to assist the development or introduction of a new or improved multichannel video programming or other service offered over multichannel video programming systems, technology, or products.\nL168: Such waiver requests should be made pursuant to cite32†§ 76.7 . Such a waiver shall be effective for all service providers and products in the category in which the waiver is granted.\nL169: #### § 76.1208 Sunset of regulations.\nL170: The regulations adopted under this subpart shall cease to apply when the Commission determines that (1) the market for multichannel video distributors is fully competitive; (2) the market for converter boxes, and interactive communications equipment, used in conjunction with that service is fully competitive; and (3) elimination of the regulations would promote competition and the public interest. Any interested party may petition the Commission for such a determination.\nL171: #### § 76.1209 Theft of service.\nL172: \nL173: Nothing in this subpart shall be construed to authorize or justify any use, manufacture, or importation of equipment that would violate cite33†47 U.S.C. 553†www.govinfo.gov or any other provision of law intended to preclude the unauthorized reception of multichannel video programming service.\nL174: #### § 76.1210 Effect on other rules.\nL175: \nL176: Nothing in this subpart affects cite34†§ 64.702(d) of the Commission's regulations or other Commission regulations governing interconnection and competitive provision of customer premises equipment used in connection with basic common carrier communications services.\nL177: \nL178: ###### eCFR Content\nL179: \nL180: ## Pages\nL181: \nL182:   * cite1†Home L183:   * cite1†Titles L184:   * cite35†Search L185:   * cite4†Recent Changes L186:   * cite36†Corrections L187: ## Reader Aids\nL188: \nL189:   * cite11†Using the eCFR Point-in-Time System L190:   * cite12†Understanding the eCFR L191:   * cite13†Government Policy and OFR Procedures L192:   * cite14†Developer Resources L193:   * cite15†Recent Site Updates L194: \nL195: ## Information\nL196: \nL197:   * cite37†About This Site L198:   * cite38†Legal Status L199:   * cite39†Privacy L200:   * cite40†Accessibility L201:   * cite41†FOIA L202:   * cite42†No Fear Act†www.archives.gov L203:   * cite43†Continuity Information†www.archives.gov L204: \nL205: ## My eCFR\nL206: \nL207:   * cite44†My Subscriptions L208:   * cite16†Sign In / Sign Up --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\neCFR :: 47 CFR Part 76 Subpart T -- Notices (https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-47/chapter-I/subchapter-C/part-76/subpart-T)\nciteturn2420view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-47/chapter-I/subchapter-C/part-76/subpart-T\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 310\nL0: Site Feedback\nL1: \nL2: ##### You are using an unsupported browser\nL3: \nL4: You are using an unsupported browser. This web site is designed for the current versions of Microsoft Edge, Google C